// Domain 5: Networking and Content Delivery (18%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d5-01", d: 5,
  q: "Instances in a private subnet cannot download operating system updates from the internet. A NAT gateway was created in the same private subnet, and the private subnet's route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to that NAT gateway.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to fix the problem?",
  o: ["Create the NAT gateway in a public subnet that routes to an internet gateway, and point the private route at it.",
      "Assign an Elastic IP address to each instance in the private subnet so that it can reach the internet directly.",
      "Add an inbound rule to the instance security group that allows all traffic from 0.0.0.0/0 on every port.",
      "Change the private subnet's route for 0.0.0.0/0 to the VPC's local route instead of to the NAT gateway."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A public zonal NAT gateway must be in a public subnet whose route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway. A NAT gateway inside the private subnet has no path to the internet, so its traffic goes nowhere. A regional NAT gateway is another option that does not need a public subnet.",
      "Instances in a subnet without a route to an internet gateway cannot use public IP addresses to reach the internet, and the design is meant to keep them private.",
      "Security groups are stateful, so return traffic for outbound requests is already allowed. Opening inbound access adds risk and does not create a route.",
      "The local route covers only traffic within the VPC's CIDR range. It cannot send traffic to the internet."] },

{ id: "d5-02", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer launches a web server in a subnet whose route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway. The instance's security group allows HTTP from 0.0.0.0/0. Users still cannot reach the server from the internet.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["The instance does not have a public IPv4 address or an Elastic IP address.",
      "The subnet's default network ACL allows all inbound and outbound traffic.",
      "The VPC does not have a NAT gateway for inbound traffic from the internet.",
      "The security group needs an outbound rule that allows the HTTP responses."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An instance needs a public IPv4 address or an Elastic IP address to be reached from the internet through an internet gateway. If auto-assign public IP is turned off for the subnet, the instance has only a private address.",
      "The default network ACL allows all traffic, so it would not block the users.",
      "NAT gateways are for outbound connections from private subnets. They do not accept inbound connections from the internet.",
      "Security groups are stateful, so responses to allowed inbound requests are allowed automatically."] },

{ id: "d5-03", d: 5,
  q: "A custom network ACL on a public subnet allows inbound TCP 443 from 0.0.0.0/0 and outbound TCP 443 to 0.0.0.0/0, and it denies all other traffic. Clients cannot load pages from a web server in the subnet, and their connections time out.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer change?",
  o: ["Allow outbound TCP 1024-65535 to 0.0.0.0/0 in the network ACL for return traffic.",
      "Allow inbound TCP 1024-65535 from 0.0.0.0/0 in the instance's security group.",
      "Add an outbound rule for TCP 443 to the instance's security group for responses.",
      "Move the deny rule for all traffic to the lowest rule number so that it is evaluated first."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Network ACLs are stateless, so return traffic must be allowed explicitly. Responses from the server go to the client's ephemeral port, typically in the range 1024-65535, which the outbound rules currently deny.",
      "Security groups are stateful and already allow responses. The block is in the stateless network ACL.",
      "Security groups track connections, so responses to allowed inbound traffic do not need outbound rules.",
      "Rules are evaluated from the lowest number up, so a deny-all rule at the top would block all traffic, including HTTPS."] },

{ id: "d5-04", d: 5,
  q: "A three-tier application has web, app, and database tiers, and the app tier runs in an Auto Scaling group whose instance IP addresses change often. The database security group must allow MySQL traffic only from the app tier instances.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer configure the database security group?",
  o: ["Add an inbound rule for port 3306 with the app tier's security group as the source.",
      "Add an inbound rule for port 3306 with the app subnet's CIDR blocks as the source.",
      "Add a network ACL rule that allows port 3306 from each app instance's current IP address.",
      "Add an inbound rule for port 3306 from 0.0.0.0/0, and rely on the private subnets."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A security group rule can use another security group as its source. Any instance that has the app tier's security group is allowed, whatever its IP address, so scaling events need no rule changes.",
      "CIDR blocks allow every resource in those subnets, not only the app tier, and they need updates if the subnets change.",
      "Per-instance IP rules break whenever instances are replaced, and network ACLs have a small rule limit.",
      "0.0.0.0/0 allows any source that can route to the database, which is far broader than required."] },

{ id: "d5-05", d: 5,
  q: "A VPC has IPv6 turned on. Instances in a private subnet must reach IPv6 software repositories on the internet, but no inbound IPv6 connections from the internet can be allowed to reach them.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer configure?",
  o: ["An egress-only internet gateway with a ::/0 route from the private subnet",
      "A NAT gateway with an IPv6 route from the private subnet to ::/0",
      "An internet gateway with a ::/0 route and a network ACL that blocks inbound",
      "A gateway VPC endpoint for the repositories with an IPv6 prefix list"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An egress-only internet gateway allows outbound IPv6 traffic and the responses to it, but it blocks connections that start from the internet. It is the IPv6 equivalent of outbound-only access through a NAT gateway.",
      "NAT gateways translate IPv4 addresses (and support NAT64 to reach IPv4 services). They are not used for IPv6-to-IPv6 egress.",
      "An internet gateway allows inbound IPv6 connections. A stateless network ACL that blocks inbound traffic would also block the responses unless it is carefully tuned, which is fragile.",
      "Gateway VPC endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB, not for general internet repositories."] },

{ id: "d5-06", d: 5,
  q: "A VPC has private subnets in three Availability Zones, and all of them route through a single NAT gateway in one zone. When that zone had an outage, instances in the other two zones lost internet access.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to prevent this?",
  o: ["Create a NAT gateway in each Availability Zone, and route each private subnet to its own zone's gateway.",
      "Add a second Elastic IP address to the existing NAT gateway so that it can fail over to another zone.",
      "Replace the NAT gateway with a NAT instance in an Auto Scaling group that has a desired capacity of one.",
      "Add an internet gateway route to the private subnets for use when the NAT gateway is unavailable."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A zonal NAT gateway runs in one Availability Zone, so it is lost with that zone. One NAT gateway per zone, with each private route table pointing to the gateway in the same zone, keeps the other zones online and avoids cross-zone data charges. NAT gateways now also offer a regional availability mode that spans zones automatically.",
      "Elastic IP addresses do not move a NAT gateway out of its Availability Zone.",
      "A NAT instance is a server to patch and scale, and it is still a single point of failure while it is being replaced.",
      "An internet gateway route makes the subnets public, and instances without public IP addresses still could not use it."] },

{ id: "d5-07", d: 5,
  q: "A VPC with the CIDR block 10.0.0.0/24 has run out of IP addresses. The company cannot re-create the VPC because many resources depend on it.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Associate a secondary IPv4 CIDR block with the VPC, and create new subnets in it.",
      "Change the VPC's primary CIDR block from /24 to /16 by editing the VPC settings.",
      "Resize the existing subnets to larger CIDR blocks while the instances keep running.",
      "Delete the five reserved addresses in each subnet so that they can be assigned again."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["You can add secondary IPv4 CIDR blocks to an existing VPC, within the allowed ranges, and create new subnets in them. Existing resources keep working, and new resources use the new subnets.",
      "A VPC's primary CIDR block cannot be changed after the VPC is created.",
      "A subnet's CIDR block cannot be changed after the subnet is created.",
      "AWS reserves five addresses in every subnet, and they cannot be released."] },

{ id: "d5-08", d: 5,
  q: "A company with 80 accounts often creates VPCs whose CIDR blocks overlap, which blocks later peering and Transit Gateway connections. The network team wants non-overlapping CIDR blocks allocated automatically and wants to track IP address use across the organization.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use Amazon VPC IP Address Manager (IPAM) with pools that allocate CIDR blocks to new VPCs.",
      "Keep a shared spreadsheet of CIDR blocks, and ask teams to check it before they create VPCs.",
      "Create every VPC with the same 10.0.0.0/16 block, and use NAT for all traffic between VPCs.",
      "Use AWS Config to delete any VPC whose CIDR block overlaps with an existing VPC in the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["IPAM organizes address space into pools across the organization and allocates non-overlapping CIDR blocks to new VPCs, including VPCs created by CloudFormation. It also monitors IP use and reports overlapping or unused addresses.",
      "A manual spreadsheet is error prone and does not enforce anything.",
      "Identical CIDR blocks make routing between VPCs impossible without complex NAT, which is the problem the team wants to avoid.",
      "Deleting VPCs after they are created is disruptive, and Config would not prevent the overlap."] },

{ id: "d5-09", d: 5,
  q: "A partner accepts API calls only from a fixed list of IP addresses. The company's application runs on instances in private subnets that use a NAT gateway for outbound traffic.\n\nWhich IP address should the company give to the partner?",
  o: ["The Elastic IP address that is associated with the NAT gateway",
      "The private IP addresses that are assigned to each application instance",
      "The public IP address of the VPC's internet gateway",
      "The CIDR block of the private subnets in the VPC"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Traffic from the private subnets leaves through the NAT gateway, which replaces the source address with its Elastic IP address. The partner sees only that address, so allowlisting it covers every instance behind the NAT gateway.",
      "Private addresses are not routable on the internet, and the partner never sees them.",
      "An internet gateway does not have its own public IP address. It translates addresses that belong to resources.",
      "Private CIDR blocks are not visible on the internet."] },

{ id: "d5-10", d: 5,
  q: "Applications in private subnets upload large amounts of data to Amazon S3 in the same Region through a NAT gateway, and NAT gateway data processing charges are high. The security team also wants this path to reach only the company's own buckets.\n\nWhich combination of actions will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a gateway VPC endpoint for S3, and associate it with the private subnets' route tables.",
      "Attach an endpoint policy that allows access only to the company's bucket ARNs.",
      "Create an interface VPC endpoint for S3 in each subnet to remove all charges for S3 traffic.",
      "Move the NAT gateway to a larger size so that it processes data at a lower price per GB.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration so that uploads bypass the NAT gateway automatically."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A gateway endpoint for S3 adds a route that uses the S3 prefix list, so traffic to S3 in the Region bypasses the NAT gateway. Gateway endpoints have no hourly or data processing charge.",
      "Endpoint policies control which actions and resources can be reached through the endpoint. Allowing only the company's bucket ARNs stops data from being sent to other buckets through this path.",
      "Interface endpoints for S3 have hourly and data processing charges. They are useful for on-premises access, but the gateway endpoint is the cheaper fix here.",
      "NAT gateways do not have sizes, and data processing is charged per GB.",
      "Transfer Acceleration uses edge locations and still sends traffic from private subnets through the NAT gateway, and it adds charges."] },

{ id: "d5-11", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer creates an interface VPC endpoint for Amazon SQS with private DNS turned on. Instances in the VPC still resolve sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com to public IP addresses, and their requests go through the NAT gateway.\n\nWhat should the engineer check FIRST?",
  o: ["That the VPC has both the enableDnsSupport and enableDnsHostnames attributes turned on.",
      "That the endpoint's security group allows inbound UDP port 53 from the instance subnets.",
      "That the SQS queue policy allows access only from the endpoint's private IP addresses.",
      "That a gateway VPC endpoint for SQS is attached to each of the private subnets' route tables."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Private DNS for interface endpoints relies on the Amazon-provided DNS resolver and a private hosted zone that AWS manages. Both enableDnsSupport and enableDnsHostnames must be true on the VPC for the service name to resolve to the endpoint's private addresses.",
      "DNS queries go to the VPC resolver, not to the endpoint, so the endpoint does not need to allow port 53. It needs to allow HTTPS from clients.",
      "A queue policy controls access, but it does not change how the name resolves.",
      "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB."] },

{ id: "d5-12", d: 5,
  q: "A SaaS provider must offer a TCP service to 200 customer VPCs in other AWS accounts. Many customer VPCs use CIDR blocks that overlap with the provider's VPC. Customers must not get access to any other resources in the provider's VPC.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Put the service behind a Network Load Balancer, and share it as a PrivateLink endpoint service.",
      "Create a VPC peering connection with each customer VPC, and add routes to the service subnet.",
      "Attach every customer VPC to a shared transit gateway, and add routes to the service subnet.",
      "Give the service a public IP address, and ask customers to reach it through their NAT gateways."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS PrivateLink exposes a service behind a Network Load Balancer as an endpoint service. Customers create interface endpoints in their own VPCs, traffic flows only to the service, and overlapping CIDR blocks do not matter because there is no routing between the VPCs.",
      "VPC peering does not work with overlapping CIDR blocks, and peering gives broader network access than one service.",
      "Transit Gateway routing also fails with overlapping CIDR blocks, and it exposes more of the network.",
      "Public exposure sends traffic over the internet and opens the service to anyone who can reach it."] },

{ id: "d5-13", d: 5,
  q: "VPC A is peered with VPC B, and VPC B is peered with VPC C. Instances in VPC A cannot reach instances in VPC C, even though all route tables have routes for the peering connections that they use.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to allow traffic between VPC A and VPC C?",
  o: ["Create a peering connection between VPC A and VPC C, and add routes in both VPCs.",
      "Add a route in VPC A that sends VPC C's CIDR block to the A-to-B peering connection.",
      "Turn on DNS resolution for the existing peering connections in both of the directions.",
      "Allow VPC C's CIDR block in the security groups of the instances in VPC A and VPC B."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["VPC peering is not transitive, so traffic cannot pass through VPC B to reach VPC C. A direct peering connection between A and C, with routes on both sides and matching security group rules, connects them. For many VPCs, a transit gateway is easier to manage.",
      "VPC B does not forward traffic between peering connections, so this route does not work.",
      "DNS resolution settings help resolve private hostnames. They do not make peering transitive.",
      "Security groups allow traffic only when a route exists, and there is no route from A to C."] },

{ id: "d5-14", d: 5,
  q: "A company connects 30 VPCs to a transit gateway. Production VPCs must not be able to reach development VPCs, but every VPC must be able to reach a shared services VPC.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer configure the transit gateway?",
  o: ["Use separate TGW route tables for production and development that both propagate the shared VPC.",
      "Use one TGW route table for every attachment, and block the traffic with network ACLs in each of the VPCs.",
      "Create a separate transit gateway for each VPC, and peer every transit gateway with each other.",
      "Turn off default route table association, and leave the attachments without any route tables."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Transit gateway route tables control which attachments can reach each other. Production attachments associate with a production route table and development attachments with a development route table. Each of these tables has routes to the shared services VPC but not to the other environment, and the shared services table has routes to both.",
      "A single route table lets every VPC reach every other VPC, and maintaining network ACLs in 30 VPCs is error prone.",
      "Thirty transit gateways and a full mesh of peering connections add cost and complexity.",
      "Attachments without route tables cannot route traffic at all, so no VPC could reach the shared services VPC."] },

{ id: "d5-15", d: 5,
  q: "On-premises servers connect to a VPC through AWS Direct Connect with a private virtual interface. The servers must upload data to Amazon S3 privately, without traversing the public internet.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an interface VPC endpoint for S3, and have the servers use its endpoint-specific DNS names.",
      "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for S3, and add a route to it in each of the on-premises router tables.",
      "Route the on-premises traffic through a NAT gateway in the VPC to reach the public S3 endpoint.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration, and send the uploads to the accelerate endpoint over the link."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Interface endpoints for S3 have private IP addresses in the VPC, so on-premises servers can reach them over Direct Connect or VPN. The servers use the endpoint-specific DNS names, or Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoints with private DNS, to send S3 requests through the endpoint.",
      "Gateway endpoints work only for traffic that starts inside the VPC. They cannot be reached from on-premises networks.",
      "VPCs do not support edge-to-edge routing, so on-premises traffic cannot use the VPC's NAT gateway to reach the internet.",
      "Transfer Acceleration sends traffic over the internet to edge locations, not over the private link."] },

{ id: "d5-16", d: 5,
  q: "A company uses AWS Site-to-Site VPN with static routing, and only one of the two tunnels is configured on its customer gateway device. During AWS maintenance on that tunnel, the company lost connectivity.\n\nWhich change will provide automatic failover between the tunnels?",
  o: ["Configure both tunnels on the customer gateway, and use BGP dynamic routing for failover.",
      "Create a second customer gateway in AWS that points to the same IP address of the device.",
      "Replace the VPN with a VPC peering connection to the on-premises network for redundancy.",
      "Turn on acceleration for the VPN so that AWS keeps the single tunnel up during maintenance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Each Site-to-Site VPN connection has two tunnels that terminate on different AWS endpoints for redundancy. Configuring both tunnels and using BGP lets traffic move to the healthy tunnel automatically during maintenance or a failure.",
      "A second customer gateway resource with the same IP address does not add a tunnel on the device, so the single-tunnel problem remains.",
      "VPC peering connects VPCs only. It cannot connect to an on-premises network.",
      "Accelerated VPN improves performance through AWS Global Accelerator. It does not keep a tunnel up during maintenance."] },

{ id: "d5-17", d: 5,
  q: "A login page behind an Application Load Balancer receives credential stuffing attacks in which single IP addresses send thousands of requests per minute. Normal users send fewer than 20 login requests in 5 minutes.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Add an AWS WAF rate-based rule, scoped down to the login path, that blocks IPs over a limit.",
      "Add a security group rule on the ALB that denies the attacking IP addresses as they appear.",
      "Turn on AWS Shield Standard for the ALB so that it blocks each of the credential stuffing attempts.",
      "Increase the ALB idle timeout so that attackers cannot open as many connections per minute."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A rate-based rule counts requests from each IP address over a time window and blocks addresses that exceed the limit. A scope-down statement limits the count to the login path, so normal browsing is not affected. The AWS WAF Fraud Control account takeover prevention rule group adds more protection.",
      "Security groups do not support deny rules, and tracking attacker IP addresses by hand cannot keep up.",
      "Shield Standard protects against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks. It does not inspect login requests.",
      "A longer idle timeout keeps connections open longer, which does not limit requests."] },

{ id: "d5-18", d: 5,
  q: "A team plans to add the AWS Managed Rules core rule set to the web ACL of a production application. The team is worried that some of the rules might block legitimate traffic.\n\nHow should the team roll out the rule group safely?",
  o: ["Add the rule group with its action overridden to Count, review the logs, and then switch to Block.",
      "Add the rule group in Block mode during a weekend, and remove it if users complain on Monday.",
      "Add the rule group to a web ACL that is not associated with any resource, and then wait for one week.",
      "Add the rule group, and change the web ACL's default action from Allow to Block at the same time."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Overriding rule actions to Count lets AWS WAF record which requests would match without blocking them. After reviewing WAF logs and sampled requests, the team can exclude or scope down noisy rules and then turn on blocking.",
      "Blocking first risks an outage, and waiting for complaints means that real users were affected.",
      "A web ACL that is not associated with a resource sees no traffic, so it provides no data.",
      "Changing the default action to Block would block all traffic that no rule allows, which is a much bigger change."] },

{ id: "d5-19", d: 5,
  q: "After AWS WAF was turned on, some customers receive HTTP 403 errors when they submit a support form that contains code samples. The team needs to find out which rule blocks these requests.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Turn on web ACL logging, and look for the terminatingRuleId in the logs for the blocked requests.",
      "Turn on ALB access logs, and look for the WAF rule name inside the user agent field of each request.",
      "Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the ALB subnets, and look for rejected packets from the customers.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for AWS WAF so that each blocked request is recorded."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Web ACL logs record each inspected request, the action taken, and the terminatingRuleId of the rule that blocked it. Logs can go to CloudWatch Logs, Amazon S3, or Data Firehose. Sampled requests in the console also help.",
      "ALB access logs do not record which WAF rule matched, and the user agent is set by the client.",
      "The requests are blocked at layer 7 with an HTTP 403 response, so flow logs would show accepted traffic.",
      "CloudTrail records WAF configuration API calls, not the web requests that AWS WAF inspects."] },

{ id: "d5-20", d: 5,
  q: "A company's public website has been hit by large DDoS attacks that caused scaling costs to spike. The company wants 24/7 access to the AWS Shield Response Team during attacks and protection against scaling charges caused by DDoS attacks.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced, and add the website's resources as protected resources.",
      "Rely on AWS Shield Standard, which includes the Shield Response Team at no extra cost.",
      "Add AWS WAF rate-based rules, which include cost protection for DDoS scaling charges.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty, and use its findings to request credits for DDoS scaling charges."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Shield Advanced adds enhanced DDoS detection and mitigation for protected resources such as CloudFront distributions, Application Load Balancers, Global Accelerator accelerators, and Route 53 hosted zones. It includes access to the Shield Response Team and cost protection for scaling charges caused by DDoS attacks.",
      "Shield Standard protects all AWS customers against common attacks at no cost, but it does not include the Shield Response Team or cost protection.",
      "Rate-based rules help with application layer floods, but AWS WAF does not provide DDoS cost protection.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats in your account. It is not a DDoS protection service, and it does not provide credits."] },

{ id: "d5-21", d: 5,
  q: "A company must restrict outbound internet traffic from its private subnets to an approved list of domain names, such as updates.example.com, and block everything else. The traffic currently leaves through a NAT gateway.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Deploy AWS Network Firewall with a stateful domain list rule group, and route egress traffic through it.",
      "Add security group outbound rules that allow the approved domain names and deny all of the other traffic.",
      "Add network ACL rules that allow the approved domain names and deny all other outbound traffic.",
      "Create a Route 53 private hosted zone for each approved domain so that only those domain names can resolve."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Network Firewall supports stateful domain list rules that inspect the TLS SNI and HTTP Host header to allow or deny traffic by domain name. Routing the private subnets' egress through firewall endpoints before the NAT gateway enforces the allow list.",
      "Security groups work with IP addresses, CIDR blocks, prefix lists, and other security groups. They cannot filter by domain name, and they have no deny rules.",
      "Network ACLs filter by IP address and port only.",
      "Private hosted zones answer for the names that they contain. They do not stop queries for other names or connections to other IP addresses."] },

{ id: "d5-22", d: 5,
  q: "The security team wants to stop EC2 instances in every VPC in the account from resolving domain names that are known to be used for malware and botnet command and control. The company does not want to maintain domain lists by hand.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall with AWS managed domain lists associated with each VPC.",
      "Add a network ACL rule that denies outbound traffic on port 53 from every subnet in each VPC.",
      "Turn on GuardDuty so that it blocks DNS queries for malicious domains from the instances.",
      "Create a private hosted zone named com that returns 0.0.0.0 for every malicious domain name."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DNS Firewall filters DNS queries that go through Route 53 Resolver in your VPCs. AWS managed domain lists, such as lists of malware and botnet domains, are kept up to date by AWS, and a rule group can block or alert on queries that match them.",
      "Blocking all DNS traffic would break every name lookup, not only the malicious ones.",
      "GuardDuty detects DNS activity linked to threats and reports findings, but it does not block queries.",
      "A hosted zone named com would break resolution of every .com domain, and keeping it current by hand is the work the team wants to avoid."] },

{ id: "d5-23", d: 5,
  q: "A company's bill shows high regional data transfer charges. Application instances in us-east-1a send most of their internet traffic to a NAT gateway in us-east-1b and read from database replicas in other zones. The company must keep its Multi-AZ design.\n\nWhich change will MOST reduce these data transfer charges?",
  o: ["Route each zone's traffic to a NAT gateway in the same zone, and prefer same-zone database replicas.",
      "Move all instances and databases into one Availability Zone so that no cross-zone traffic remains.",
      "Turn on cross-zone load balancing on every load balancer so that traffic spreads evenly across zones.",
      "Replace the NAT gateway with an internet gateway route in the private subnets of us-east-1a."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data transfer between Availability Zones in the same Region is charged in each direction. Sending each zone's internet-bound traffic through a NAT gateway in the same zone, and reading from replicas in the same zone where possible, removes most cross-zone charges while keeping the Multi-AZ design.",
      "One zone removes the charges but breaks the Multi-AZ requirement, so the application would fail if that zone fails.",
      "Cross-zone load balancing spreads traffic across zones, which can increase cross-zone data transfer.",
      "An internet gateway route would make the subnets public, and instances without public IP addresses still could not use it."] },

{ id: "d5-24", d: 5,
  q: "After AWS began charging for all public IPv4 addresses, a company's VPC costs went up. The company has many Elastic IP addresses that are not associated with any resource. It also has many instances with public IP addresses that are reached only through a load balancer.\n\nWhich combination of actions will reduce these costs? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Release the Elastic IP addresses that are not associated with any resource.",
      "Move instances behind the load balancer to private subnets without public IPs.",
      "Replace each public IPv4 address with a second Elastic IP address on the instance.",
      "Turn on auto-assign public IPv4 addresses for every subnet in the VPC by default.",
      "Move the load balancer into private subnets so that it no longer needs public IPs."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["AWS charges for every public IPv4 address, whether it is in use or idle. Releasing Elastic IP addresses that are not associated with anything removes their charge. IPAM public IP insights can help find them.",
      "Instances that are reached only through a load balancer do not need public addresses. Placing them in private subnets, with NAT for outbound access if needed, removes their public IPv4 charges and reduces exposure.",
      "Elastic IP addresses are also public IPv4 addresses and are charged the same way.",
      "Auto-assigning public addresses adds more public IPv4 addresses and more cost.",
      "An internet-facing load balancer must be in public subnets with public addresses to receive internet traffic, so this would take the site offline."] },

{ id: "d5-25", d: 5,
  q: "A media company serves large video files directly from an S3 bucket to viewers around the world. Data transfer out from S3 to the internet is the largest item on the bill, and many viewers request the same files.\n\nWhich solution will reduce data transfer costs?",
  o: ["Serve the files through a CloudFront distribution with the S3 bucket as its origin.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration so that downloads go through the AWS edge network.",
      "Move the files to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval to lower the cost of each download.",
      "Create S3 Replication copies in three Regions, and let viewers pick the nearest one."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data transfer from Amazon S3 to CloudFront is free, and CloudFront serves repeated requests from edge caches. CloudFront data transfer out pricing is usually lower than S3 data transfer to the internet, so caching popular files reduces cost and improves performance.",
      "Transfer Acceleration adds a per-GB charge on top of normal data transfer, so it increases cost.",
      "Glacier Instant Retrieval lowers storage cost but adds retrieval charges, and data transfer out is still charged.",
      "Extra copies add storage and replication costs, and downloads still incur S3 data transfer out charges."] },

{ id: "d5-26", d: 5,
  q: "Two VPCs in the same Region exchange 200 TB of data each month through a transit gateway. These are the only two VPCs that need to communicate with each other. The company wants to reduce the cost of this traffic.\n\nWhich solution will MOST reduce the cost?",
  o: ["Replace the transit gateway path with a VPC peering connection between the two VPCs.",
      "Add a second transit gateway attachment to each VPC so that data processing is shared.",
      "Route the traffic through the internet by using public IP addresses on both sides instead.",
      "Create a Site-to-Site VPN between the two VPCs, and send the traffic over the tunnels."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Transit Gateway charges per attachment hour and per GB of data processed. VPC peering has no hourly charge and no data processing charge, and peering traffic that stays within one Availability Zone has no data transfer charge. For two VPCs with heavy traffic, peering is cheaper.",
      "More attachments add hourly charges, and every GB is still processed by the transit gateway.",
      "Sending traffic through public addresses adds data transfer charges and exposes the traffic.",
      "VPN connections between VPCs add hourly charges, have lower throughput, and need more management."] },

{ id: "d5-27", d: 5,
  q: "A private hosted zone named internal.example.com was created in account A and is associated with a VPC in account A. Instances in a VPC in account B must resolve its records, but their queries return NXDOMAIN.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Authorize the association in account A, and then associate the zone with the VPC from account B.",
      "Share the private hosted zone with account B by using AWS RAM, and accept the share in account B.",
      "Create a public hosted zone with the same name so that both accounts can resolve the records.",
      "Create a VPC peering connection between the VPCs so that DNS queries are forwarded to account A."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A private hosted zone can be associated with a VPC in another account. An administrator in account A creates a VPC association authorization, and an administrator in account B then associates the VPC with the zone. The VPC must have DNS resolution and DNS hostnames turned on.",
      "Private hosted zones themselves are not shared through AWS RAM, so cross-account association uses an association authorization. Route 53 Profiles, which can be shared through AWS RAM, are another way to apply DNS settings at scale.",
      "A public zone would expose the internal records to the internet.",
      "Peering does not make a private hosted zone available to the other VPC. The zone must be associated with that VPC."] },

{ id: "d5-28", d: 5,
  q: "Applications in a VPC must resolve hostnames in the on-premises domain corp.local, which only the on-premises DNS servers know. The VPC is connected to the data center through AWS Direct Connect.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer configure?",
  o: ["A Resolver outbound endpoint with a forwarding rule for corp.local to the on-premises DNS servers",
      "A Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint that the on-premises DNS servers are able to forward queries to",
      "A private hosted zone named corp.local with a copy of each on-premises DNS record in it",
      "A DHCP options set that points the VPC only at the on-premises DNS server IP addresses"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An outbound endpoint lets Route 53 Resolver send queries out of the VPC. A forwarding rule for corp.local sends matching queries to the on-premises DNS servers over Direct Connect, while all other queries are still resolved by Route 53 Resolver.",
      "Inbound endpoints handle the opposite direction: they let on-premises servers resolve names in AWS.",
      "Copying records by hand goes out of date and duplicates the on-premises DNS data.",
      "Sending all queries to on-premises servers breaks resolution of AWS private names and makes every lookup depend on the hybrid link."] },

{ id: "d5-29", d: 5,
  q: "On-premises servers must resolve records in a Route 53 private hosted zone named aws.example.com that is associated with a VPC. The data center connects to the VPC through a Site-to-Site VPN.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create a Resolver inbound endpoint in the VPC, and forward aws.example.com from on-premises DNS to it.",
      "Create a Resolver outbound endpoint in the VPC, and associate a forwarding rule for aws.example.com.",
      "Change the private hosted zone into a public hosted zone so that on-premises servers can query it online.",
      "Point the on-premises servers directly at the Amazon DNS server address at the VPC base plus two."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An inbound endpoint gives Route 53 Resolver IP addresses in the VPC that on-premises DNS servers can reach over the VPN. A conditional forwarder for aws.example.com on the on-premises servers sends those queries to the endpoint, which answers from the private hosted zone.",
      "Outbound endpoints send queries from the VPC to other DNS servers, which is the opposite direction.",
      "A private hosted zone cannot be converted into a public zone, and publishing internal records is a security risk.",
      "The Amazon-provided DNS server cannot be reached from outside the VPC, so on-premises servers cannot query it directly."] },

{ id: "d5-30", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs the same application in us-east-1, eu-west-1, and ap-southeast-1. Users around the world must be sent to the Region that gives them the fastest response, and traffic must avoid any Region that becomes unhealthy.\n\nWhich Route 53 configuration should a CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["Latency-based records for each Region, with health checks associated",
      "Geolocation records for each continent, with a default record for others",
      "Weighted records with equal weights for each Region and no health checks",
      "A simple record with all three load balancer addresses as its values"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Latency-based routing answers with the Region that has the lowest measured network latency for the user. With health checks, Route 53 skips an unhealthy Region and answers with the next best one.",
      "Geolocation routes by the user's location, not by measured latency, so some users might not get the fastest Region.",
      "Equal weights spread users across Regions regardless of latency, and without health checks a failed Region keeps receiving traffic.",
      "A simple record with several values returns them in random order, with no latency awareness or health checks."] },

{ id: "d5-31", d: 5,
  q: "Because of a licensing agreement, users in Germany must be served by an application stack in eu-central-1, and all other users must be served by a stack in us-east-1. Users whose location cannot be determined must still be served.\n\nWhich Route 53 configuration will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Geolocation routing with a Germany record for eu-central-1 and a Default record for us-east-1",
      "Latency-based routing with one record for eu-central-1 and one record for us-east-1",
      "Geoproximity routing with a large positive bias on the eu-central-1 record and none on us-east-1",
      "Geolocation routing with only a Germany record that points to eu-central-1"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Geolocation routing answers based on where the DNS query comes from. A record for Germany sends those users to eu-central-1, and the Default record covers every other location, including queries whose location cannot be determined.",
      "Latency routing would send some users outside Germany to eu-central-1, and some German users could go elsewhere, which breaks the licensing rule.",
      "Geoproximity shifts traffic by distance and bias, so the boundary would not match the borders of Germany.",
      "Without a Default record, Route 53 returns no answer for users outside Germany or for unknown locations."] },

{ id: "d5-32", d: 5,
  q: "A company is migrating a website from an on-premises server to an Application Load Balancer on AWS. The company wants to send 10% of users to AWS first, increase the share gradually, and move users back if errors appear.\n\nWhich Route 53 routing policy should a CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["Weighted routing",
      "Failover routing",
      "Multivalue answer routing",
      "IP-based routing"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Weighted routing splits DNS responses between records in proportion to their weights, such as 90 and 10. Changing the weights shifts traffic gradually, and setting the AWS record's weight to 0 moves users back. A low TTL makes changes take effect faster.",
      "Failover routing sends all traffic to the primary while it is healthy, so it cannot send a percentage of users.",
      "Multivalue answer returns several healthy records for clients to choose from. It does not control the share of traffic.",
      "IP-based routing sends users based on their source IP ranges, not by percentage."] },

{ id: "d5-33", d: 5,
  q: "A small internal service runs on 6 EC2 instances without a load balancer. The company wants DNS to return up to 8 healthy instance IP addresses at random so that clients spread their requests. Unhealthy instances must not be returned.\n\nWhich Route 53 configuration should a CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["Multivalue answer records for each instance, each with its own health check",
      "A simple record that lists all 6 instance IP addresses as values of the same record",
      "Failover records, with one primary record and five secondary records",
      "Latency-based records for each instance in the same AWS Region"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multivalue answer routing returns up to 8 healthy records, chosen at random, for each query. A health check on each record removes unhealthy instances from the responses. It does not replace a load balancer, but it improves availability for simple cases.",
      "A simple record returns all of its values without health checks, so clients can still receive the addresses of failed instances.",
      "Failover routing supports one primary and one secondary record, and it does not spread traffic across instances.",
      "Latency routing chooses between Regions. Records in the same Region do not spread traffic in a useful way."] },

{ id: "d5-34", d: 5,
  q: "A company wants both example.com (the zone apex) and www.example.com to point to an Application Load Balancer. A CloudOps engineer tries to create a CNAME record for example.com, and Route 53 rejects it.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Create an alias A record for example.com that targets the ALB.",
      "Create an A record for example.com with the ALB's current IP addresses.",
      "Create an MX record for example.com that points to the ALB's DNS name.",
      "Create a TXT record for example.com that contains the ALB's DNS name."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DNS does not allow a CNAME record at the zone apex. A Route 53 alias record can point the apex to AWS resources such as an ALB, a CloudFront distribution, or an S3 website endpoint. It follows IP address changes automatically, and queries to alias records for AWS resources have no charge.",
      "ALB IP addresses change over time, so a static A record would break.",
      "MX records direct email delivery. They do not route web traffic.",
      "TXT records hold text values, such as verification strings. Browsers do not use them to find a website."] },

{ id: "d5-35", d: 5,
  q: "The security team wants to log every DNS query that public resolvers send to Route 53 for the company's public hosted zone. A CloudOps engineer tries to set up query logging to a log group in eu-west-1, but the log group cannot be selected.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Create the log group in us-east-1, and add a resource policy that allows Route 53 to write to it.",
      "Turn on Resolver query logging for the VPC in eu-west-1 so that public zone queries are captured.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for Route 53 so that every public DNS query is recorded in the trail.",
      "Change the hosted zone to a private hosted zone so that query logging can use any of the Regions."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Query logging for public hosted zones sends logs to CloudWatch Logs in the US East (N. Virginia) Region. The log group must be in us-east-1, and a CloudWatch Logs resource policy must allow Route 53 to publish to it.",
      "Resolver query logging records queries made from inside your VPCs. It does not capture queries from public resolvers on the internet.",
      "CloudTrail records Route 53 API calls, such as record changes, not DNS queries.",
      "Changing the zone type would make the domain unreachable from the internet."] },

{ id: "d5-36", d: 5,
  q: "A company will move a website to a new IP address next week. The current A record has a TTL of 86,400 seconds. The company wants users to reach the new address within minutes after the change.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Lower the TTL to 60 seconds at least a day before the change, and update the record during the cutover.",
      "Keep the TTL at 86,400 seconds, and ask each public DNS resolver to flush its cache after the cutover is done.",
      "Raise the TTL to 172,800 seconds before the change so that resolvers keep the record for longer.",
      "Delete the record during the cutover, and create a new record with the same name a day later."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Resolvers cache a record for its TTL. Lowering the TTL in advance, by at least as long as the old TTL, makes sure that cached copies expire quickly. After the cutover, the TTL can be raised again.",
      "You cannot flush the caches of public resolvers that you do not control.",
      "A longer TTL keeps the old address cached for even longer.",
      "Deleting the record causes failed lookups for a full day."] },

{ id: "d5-37", d: 5,
  q: "A static website is served from an S3 bucket through a CloudFront distribution. Users can also download files directly from the bucket's URL, which bypasses CloudFront and its AWS WAF rules. The bucket uses SSE-KMS encryption.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer make the files reachable only through CloudFront?",
  o: ["Use origin access control, and allow only the distribution's ARN in the bucket policy and key policy.",
      "Turn on S3 static website hosting, and allow public read access only for the CloudFront edge IP ranges.",
      "Use an origin access identity, and turn off Block Public Access so that CloudFront can sign requests.",
      "Add a CloudFront geo restriction that blocks every country except the countries where the users live."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Origin access control (OAC) lets CloudFront sign its requests to the S3 origin. A bucket policy that allows the cloudfront.amazonaws.com service principal only when AWS:SourceArn is the distribution's ARN blocks direct access. OAC supports SSE-KMS, so the key policy must also allow the distribution.",
      "Public read access, even if it is scoped to IP ranges, keeps the bucket publicly reachable and is hard to maintain.",
      "Origin access identity is the legacy method and does not support SSE-KMS, and turning off Block Public Access is unnecessary and risky.",
      "Geo restriction applies to requests that go through CloudFront. It does not stop direct requests to the bucket."] },

{ id: "d5-38", d: 5,
  q: "A training company sells video courses. Each course has hundreds of video files under one path in CloudFront. Only paying customers may watch them, and customers must not be able to share links that work forever.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST effort for the application?",
  o: ["Use CloudFront signed cookies with an expiration time for the course path.",
      "Use CloudFront signed URLs for each of the hundreds of files in a course.",
      "Make the S3 bucket public, and change the object names every few days.",
      "Turn on CloudFront geo restriction for the countries of paying customers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Signed cookies grant access to many files, such as everything under one path, with one set of cookies. The policy can set an expiration time, so shared access stops working after it expires.",
      "Signed URLs work, but the application would have to create a signed URL for every file, which is more effort when a course has hundreds of files.",
      "A public bucket can be accessed by anyone who finds an object name, and renaming files does not control access.",
      "Geo restriction controls access by country, not by whether a user has paid."] },

{ id: "d5-39", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudFront distribution serves images from an S3 bucket in us-east-1. The company replicates the bucket to us-west-2 and wants CloudFront to serve images from us-west-2 automatically if the primary bucket returns errors.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer configure?",
  o: ["An origin group with the primary bucket, the secondary bucket, and failover status codes",
      "Two distributions, and a Route 53 failover record that points to the second one when needed",
      "A Lambda@Edge function that copies each failed image from us-west-2 into us-east-1 on demand",
      "A longer default TTL so that images stay in the cache for longer when the primary bucket fails"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An origin group has a primary and a secondary origin. When the primary returns one of the configured status codes, such as 500, 502, 503, or 504, or the connection fails, CloudFront retries the request against the secondary origin automatically.",
      "Two distributions with DNS failover are more complex and slower, because DNS changes depend on health checks and caching.",
      "Copying files on demand is custom code, and the primary bucket would still be failing.",
      "Caching helps only for images that are already cached. New or expired requests would still fail."] },

{ id: "d5-40", d: 5,
  q: "A multiplayer game uses UDP and runs on Network Load Balancers in two Regions. Players need static IP addresses that they can allowlist in their firewalls, lower latency over long distances, and failover between Regions within seconds.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use AWS Global Accelerator with endpoint groups for the NLBs in both Regions.",
      "Use a CloudFront distribution in front of the NLBs with caching turned off.",
      "Use Route 53 latency-based routing with health checks for the two NLBs.",
      "Use Elastic IP addresses on the NLBs, and ask players to switch addresses during a failure."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IP addresses, carries traffic over the AWS global network from the nearest edge location, and supports TCP and UDP. Health checks move traffic to healthy endpoint groups within seconds, without DNS caching delays.",
      "CloudFront delivers HTTP and HTTPS content. It does not proxy arbitrary UDP traffic, so it cannot front a UDP game.",
      "DNS-based routing gives no static IP addresses that cover both Regions, and failover is slowed by DNS caching.",
      "Asking players to switch addresses by hand is not automatic failover."] },

{ id: "d5-41", d: 5,
  q: "An Application Load Balancer is the origin for a CloudFront distribution that has AWS WAF rules. Users can bypass CloudFront by calling the ALB's DNS name directly. The company wants the ALB to accept only requests that come through this distribution.\n\nWhich combination of actions will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Allow inbound traffic on the ALB security group only from the CloudFront managed prefix list.",
      "Have CloudFront add a secret custom header, and add an ALB listener rule that requires it.",
      "Turn on access logs for the ALB, and block direct callers by hand when they show up in the logs.",
      "Turn on cross-zone load balancing so that requests from CloudFront are accepted in every zone.",
      "Add a CloudFront geo restriction that blocks the countries where most direct requests come from."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The AWS-managed prefix list com.amazonaws.global.cloudfront.origin-facing contains the IP ranges that CloudFront uses to reach origins. Allowing only that prefix list blocks direct requests from other sources.",
      "The prefix list covers every CloudFront distribution, including distributions that other customers own. A secret header that only your distribution adds, checked by an ALB listener rule, makes sure that requests come through your distribution. CloudFront VPC origins, which keep the ALB in private subnets, are another option.",
      "Blocking callers by hand after the fact is slow and does not prevent the bypass.",
      "Cross-zone load balancing spreads traffic across targets. It does not restrict who can reach the ALB.",
      "Geo restriction applies only to requests that come through CloudFront, so it does not affect direct calls to the ALB."] },

{ id: "d5-42", d: 5,
  q: "A company wants to redirect old URL paths to new paths and add security headers to every CloudFront response. The logic is simple, and the company wants the lowest cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["CloudFront Functions on the viewer request and viewer response events",
      "Lambda@Edge functions on the origin request and origin response events",
      "A Lambda function behind an ALB that the distribution uses as its origin",
      "An AWS WAF rule group that rewrites URLs and adds the response headers"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront Functions run lightweight JavaScript at edge locations on viewer request and viewer response events, with sub-millisecond startup and very high scale, at a lower price than Lambda@Edge. Redirects and header changes are typical uses. A response headers policy can also add security headers without code.",
      "Lambda@Edge suits heavier logic, such as network calls, but it costs more, so it is not the cheapest choice for simple logic.",
      "Sending every request to an origin function adds latency and cost and removes the benefit of edge caching.",
      "AWS WAF filters requests. It does not rewrite URLs or add response headers to responses."] },

{ id: "d5-43", d: 5,
  q: "Because of distribution rights, a video service must not serve content to viewers in three specific countries. The content is delivered through CloudFront.\n\nWhat is the simplest way to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on CloudFront geographic restrictions with a block list of those countries.",
      "Create a Route 53 geolocation record that returns no answer for those three countries.",
      "Add a bucket policy that denies requests from the IP ranges of those three countries.",
      "Write a Lambda@Edge function that looks up each viewer's country in a third-party API."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront geographic restrictions use a GeoIP database to allow or block viewers by country. A block list returns HTTP 403 to viewers in the listed countries, with no code.",
      "DNS location is based on the resolver, not the viewer, and users could still reach the distribution through other resolvers.",
      "Requests to the bucket come from CloudFront, not from viewers, and country IP ranges change often.",
      "A custom function adds latency and cost, and CloudFront already provides country-based restrictions."] },

{ id: "d5-44", d: 5,
  q: "Route 53 health checks for a public web server always report unhealthy, but the site works for users. The server's security group allows HTTPS only from the company's office and from a list of partner IP addresses.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Allow inbound HTTPS from the Route 53 health checker IP ranges in the security group.",
      "Change the health check to use the server's private IP address so that it stays inside the VPC.",
      "Turn on string matching in the health check so that it ignores connection timeouts from the server.",
      "Increase the health check failure threshold to 10 so that the check reports healthy more often."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Route 53 health checkers send requests from AWS IP ranges on the internet, so the security group must allow them. The ranges are published in ip-ranges.json under the ROUTE53_HEALTHCHECKS service, and an AWS-managed prefix list for Route 53 health checks is also available.",
      "Health checkers run on the internet and cannot reach private IP addresses.",
      "String matching checks the response body. It cannot help when the request never reaches the server.",
      "A higher threshold only delays the unhealthy status. Every check still fails."] },

{ id: "d5-45", d: 5,
  q: "Users cannot connect to an application on an EC2 instance. VPC Flow Logs for the instance's network interface show the inbound request from a user as ACCEPT, and they show the instance's response to the user's ephemeral port as REJECT.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["The subnet's network ACL denies the outbound response traffic.",
      "The instance's security group denies the inbound request traffic.",
      "The instance's security group denies the outbound response traffic.",
      "The route table has no route back to the user's IP address range."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security groups are stateful: if an inbound request is accepted, its response is allowed automatically. A rejected response therefore points to the stateless network ACL, which must allow outbound traffic to the client's ephemeral ports.",
      "The flow log shows the inbound request as ACCEPT, so it was not denied.",
      "Because security groups are stateful, an accepted inbound request means that its response is allowed, whatever the outbound rules say.",
      "A missing route would not appear as a REJECT record for the response. Flow logs show REJECT when a security group or network ACL denies traffic."] },

{ id: "d5-46", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer must find which instances in private subnets send the most traffic to the internet through a NAT gateway. Flow logs on the NAT gateway's network interface show only the NAT gateway's own IP address as the source of the internet traffic.\n\nWhich solution will identify the original source instances with the LEAST effort?",
  o: ["Use a custom flow log format with pkt-srcaddr and pkt-dstaddr, publish to S3, and query with Athena.",
      "Turn on ALB access logs for the NAT gateway, and sort the requests by the client IP field in each log.",
      "Install packet capture software on the NAT gateway, and analyze the captures each day.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for the NAT gateway so that each connection is recorded."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The pkt-srcaddr and pkt-dstaddr fields show the original packet-level addresses of traffic that passes through an intermediate device such as a NAT gateway. Publishing flow logs to Amazon S3 and querying them with Athena makes it easy to rank instances by bytes sent.",
      "NAT gateways are not load balancers and have no access logs.",
      "NAT gateways are managed by AWS, so you cannot install software on them.",
      "CloudTrail records API calls, not network connections."] },

{ id: "d5-47", d: 5,
  q: "An application on an EC2 instance in VPC A cannot connect to a database on port 5432 on an instance in VPC B. The VPCs are connected through a transit gateway, and the path involves several route tables, security groups, and network ACLs.\n\nWhich tool will MOST quickly identify the component that blocks the path?",
  o: ["VPC Reachability Analyzer",
      "Amazon Inspector network reachability findings",
      "AWS Trusted Advisor",
      "CloudWatch Synthetics"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Reachability Analyzer analyzes the network configuration between a source and a destination without sending packets. It reports whether the destination is reachable and, if it is not, which component blocks the path, such as a missing transit gateway route, a security group rule, or a network ACL rule.",
      "Inspector network reachability findings show which ports on instances are exposed to networks such as the internet. They do not trace a specific path between two resources to show what blocks it.",
      "Trusted Advisor checks for best practices. It does not analyze a specific network path.",
      "Synthetics canaries can show that a connection fails, but not which configuration component causes the failure."] },

{ id: "d5-48", d: 5,
  q: "Users report that some API calls through an Application Load Balancer take more than 10 seconds. The team needs to know whether the time is spent in the load balancer or in the backend targets, for each slow request.\n\nWhich data source should a CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["ALB access logs, comparing the request, target, and response processing time fields",
      "VPC Flow Logs for the ALB network interfaces, comparing the start and end time of each record",
      "CloudTrail management events for Elastic Load Balancing, comparing the event times of each call",
      "The ALB's HealthyHostCount metric, comparing the number of healthy targets during each request"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Each ALB access log entry records request_processing_time, target_processing_time, and response_processing_time. A large target_processing_time shows that the backend was slow, while large values in the other fields point to the load balancer or the client connection. The TargetResponseTime metric shows the same trend in aggregate.",
      "Flow logs aggregate traffic over capture windows and do not show HTTP request timings.",
      "CloudTrail records API calls that manage the load balancer, not client requests.",
      "HealthyHostCount shows how many targets are healthy. It does not show the latency of individual requests."] },

{ id: "d5-49", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudFront distribution in front of a product catalog has a cache hit ratio of 15%. The cache key includes a session cookie, tracking query strings such as utm_source, and the User-Agent header. The catalog content does not depend on any of these values.\n\nWhich change will MOST improve the cache hit ratio?",
  o: ["Use a cache policy that leaves cookies, the tracking query strings, and User-Agent out of the cache key.",
      "Lower the minimum TTL in the cache policy to 0 seconds so that CloudFront refreshes objects more often.",
      "Turn on Origin Shield in a Region far from the origin so that more edge locations share one cache.",
      "Add more origins to an origin group so that cache misses are spread across several origin servers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Every value in the cache key creates a separate cached object, so including cookies, tracking parameters, and User-Agent splits the cache into many copies of the same content. A cache policy that includes only values that change the response raises the hit ratio. An origin request policy can still forward values that the origin needs without adding them to the cache key.",
      "A lower TTL makes objects expire sooner, which lowers the hit ratio.",
      "Origin Shield can help, but it should be placed in the Region closest to the origin, and it does not fix a fragmented cache key.",
      "Origin groups are for failover. They do not change how often CloudFront finds objects in its cache."] },

{ id: "d5-50", d: 5,
  q: "After a deployment updates main.js in the S3 origin, many users still receive the old version from CloudFront for up to 24 hours, which is the file's TTL. The company wants users to get new versions right after each deployment from now on.\n\nWhich combination of actions will meet this requirement? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a CloudFront invalidation for /main.js right after the current deployment.",
      "Use versioned file names, such as main.3f2a1c.js, and update the HTML references.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for the origin bucket so that CloudFront fetches the newest version.",
      "Increase the default TTL to 7 days so that CloudFront checks the origin less often.",
      "Turn on CloudFront standard logging so that stale objects are removed from the edge."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["An invalidation removes the cached object from edge caches, so the next request fetches the new file from the origin. It fixes the current deployment, and invalidation paths beyond the monthly free allowance are charged.",
      "Versioned file names give each release a new URL that is not in any cache, so users get the new file at once while long TTLs stay in place. This is the recommended long-term approach.",
      "S3 Versioning keeps object versions in the bucket. CloudFront still serves its cached copy until the TTL expires.",
      "A longer TTL makes stale content last even longer.",
      "Logging records requests. It does not remove anything from the cache."] },

{ id: "d5-51", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudFront distribution uses a custom origin, origin.example.com, over HTTPS. After the origin team replaced the origin's certificate with a self-signed certificate, CloudFront started returning HTTP 502 errors.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Install a certificate on the origin from a trusted CA that covers origin.example.com.",
      "Increase the origin response timeout in the distribution settings from 30 to 60 seconds.",
      "Invalidate all paths in the distribution so that CloudFront fetches new copies of objects.",
      "Change the viewer protocol policy to allow HTTP so that viewers do not need certificates."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront validates the origin's certificate when it connects over HTTPS. A self-signed certificate, or one whose name does not match the origin domain, makes the TLS handshake fail, and CloudFront returns 502 Bad Gateway. A certificate from a trusted CA that covers the origin name fixes the problem.",
      "The timeout setting affects slow responses, which cause 504 errors. Here the TLS handshake fails before any response.",
      "Invalidations clear cached objects. They do not fix the connection to the origin.",
      "The viewer protocol policy controls the viewer side. The failure is on the connection from CloudFront to the origin."] },

{ id: "d5-52", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudFront distribution in front of an Application Load Balancer returns HTTP 504 errors for a reporting endpoint. The origin needs about 45 seconds to generate each report. Other endpoints work normally.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Increase the origin response timeout for the origin to more than 45 seconds.",
      "Lower the distribution's default TTL so that reports are refreshed more often.",
      "Add a geo restriction so that only users near the origin can call the endpoint.",
      "Replace the ALB origin with an S3 bucket that serves the reporting endpoint."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront waits for the origin response timeout, 30 seconds by default, before it gives up and returns 504 Gateway Timeout. Raising the timeout above 45 seconds (values above 120 seconds need a quota increase) lets the report finish. Making the report asynchronous is a better long-term fix.",
      "TTL affects caching of successful responses. It does not let slow requests finish.",
      "Distance to the origin is not the cause. The origin itself takes 45 seconds.",
      "S3 cannot run the report generation logic."] },

{ id: "d5-53", d: 5,
  q: "Static images served through CloudFront always show Miss in the X-Cache response header. The origin sends Cache-Control: no-store on every response, and the cache policy uses the origin's cache headers with a minimum TTL of 0.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to make CloudFront cache the images?",
  o: ["Change the origin to send Cache-Control: max-age for images, or set a minimum TTL in the cache policy.",
      "Turn on automatic compression in the cache policy so that the images are small enough to be cached at the edge.",
      "Add the images' query strings to the cache key so that each image request gets its own cache entry.",
      "Turn on HTTP/3 for the distribution so that the image requests can be served from the edge caches."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With a minimum TTL of 0, CloudFront follows the origin's Cache-Control headers, and no-store tells it not to cache. Sending a max-age value for images from the origin, or raising the minimum TTL in the cache policy, lets CloudFront cache them.",
      "Compression reduces object size. It does not override caching directives.",
      "Adding values to the cache key splits the cache further, and it does not change the no-store directive.",
      "HTTP/3 changes the protocol between viewers and CloudFront. It does not affect caching rules."] },

{ id: "d5-54", d: 5,
  q: "Amazon ECS tasks on AWS Fargate run in private subnets that have no NAT gateway. The tasks fail to start with an error saying that they cannot pull the container image from Amazon ECR.\n\nWhich combination of VPC endpoints is required for the tasks to pull images? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Interface VPC endpoints for com.amazonaws.region.ecr.api and ecr.dkr",
      "A gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon S3, where ECR stores image layers",
      "A gateway VPC endpoint for DynamoDB, where ECR stores image manifests",
      "An interface VPC endpoint for AWS Systems Manager, which ECS uses to pull",
      "An interface VPC endpoint for Amazon EC2, which Fargate uses for images"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Pulling an image calls the ECR API (ecr.api) for authentication and metadata and the Docker registry endpoint (ecr.dkr) for the image itself. Without internet access, the tasks need interface endpoints for both.",
      "ECR stores image layers in Amazon S3, so a gateway endpoint for S3 on the private route tables is also needed. If the tasks send logs to CloudWatch Logs, a logs interface endpoint is needed too.",
      "ECR does not store manifests in DynamoDB, and no DynamoDB endpoint is needed to pull images.",
      "Systems Manager endpoints are not used to pull container images.",
      "Fargate does not need an EC2 endpoint to pull images from ECR."] },

{ id: "d5-55", d: 5,
  q: "A Site-to-Site VPN connection uses BGP, and both tunnels show UP. On-premises hosts cannot reach instances in the VPC's private subnets. The on-premises router receives the VPC CIDR block through BGP correctly.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer check?",
  o: ["That route propagation from the virtual private gateway is turned on in the subnets' route tables.",
      "That the VPN connection uses static routing instead of BGP so that the routes are fixed and known.",
      "That the customer gateway has an Elastic IP address that is attached to an instance in the VPC.",
      "That an internet gateway is attached to the VPC so that the VPN tunnels are able to carry the return traffic."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The tunnels are up and the on-premises side has the VPC routes, so the missing piece is the return path. Turning on route propagation for the virtual private gateway in the private subnets' route tables adds the on-premises prefixes learned through BGP. Security groups and network ACLs must also allow the traffic.",
      "BGP is working, and switching to static routing would not add routes to the VPC route tables.",
      "The customer gateway is the on-premises device. It is not an Elastic IP address on an AWS instance.",
      "VPN traffic uses the virtual private gateway, not an internet gateway."] },

{ id: "d5-56", d: 5,
  q: "VPC A and VPC B are attached to a transit gateway. Both attachments are associated with the same transit gateway route table, which has propagated routes for both VPC CIDR blocks. Instances in VPC A still cannot reach VPC B.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["VPC A's subnet route tables have no route that sends VPC B's CIDR block to the transit gateway.",
      "Transit gateways do not support traffic between two VPCs that are attached to the same route table.",
      "VPC A and VPC B must be peered with each other in addition to being attached to the transit gateway.",
      "The transit gateway needs an internet gateway attachment to route traffic between the two VPCs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The transit gateway route table handles routing between attachments, but each VPC's own subnet route tables must also send traffic for the other VPC's CIDR block to the transit gateway. VPC B also needs a return route.",
      "Attachments that share a route table with routes to each other can communicate. This is a common design.",
      "Peering is not needed when both VPCs route through the transit gateway.",
      "Transit gateways route between attachments directly. Internet gateways are attached to VPCs, not to transit gateways."] },

{ id: "d5-57", d: 5,
  q: "An application team reports intermittent slowness between microservices on EC2 instances in two Availability Zones. A CloudOps engineer wants to measure TCP retransmissions, round-trip time, and data transferred between the workloads, and to see whether the AWS network is involved.\n\nWhich service should the engineer use?",
  o: ["Amazon CloudWatch Network Flow Monitor",
      "Amazon CloudWatch Internet Monitor",
      "CloudWatch Network Synthetic Monitor",
      "VPC Reachability Analyzer"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Network Flow Monitor uses lightweight agents on instances to collect TCP performance metrics, such as retransmissions, round-trip time, and data transferred, between workloads in AWS. It also shows a network health indicator that helps tell whether the AWS network is involved.",
      "Internet Monitor measures the performance and availability of internet traffic between AWS-hosted applications and end users, not traffic between workloads inside AWS.",
      "Network Synthetic Monitor sends synthetic probes from AWS to on-premises destinations over hybrid connections. It does not measure real traffic between microservices.",
      "Reachability Analyzer checks whether the configuration allows a path. It does not measure performance."] },

{ id: "d5-58", d: 5,
  q: "A global streaming company hosts its application on AWS. The company wants to know when internet problems, such as an ISP outage in a specific city, are hurting its users' latency and availability, and which users are affected.\n\nWhich service should a CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["Amazon CloudWatch Internet Monitor",
      "Amazon CloudWatch Network Flow Monitor",
      "AWS Health Dashboard",
      "VPC Flow Logs"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Internet Monitor uses AWS's view of global internet traffic to measure performance and availability between your application and its users, broken down by city, network (ASN), and Region. It creates health events when internet issues affect your users and can suggest changes, such as serving users from different Regions.",
      "Network Flow Monitor measures traffic between workloads inside AWS, not the internet path to end users.",
      "The Health Dashboard reports events that affect AWS services and your resources, not problems with third-party ISPs.",
      "Flow logs record traffic metadata for VPC network interfaces. They do not measure user experience across the internet."] },

{ id: "d5-59", d: 5,
  q: "A company connects its data center to AWS with AWS Direct Connect and a backup VPN. The company wants continuous measurements of packet loss and latency from its VPC to on-premises servers over these connections, without installing agents on the servers.\n\nWhich service should a CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["Amazon CloudWatch Network Synthetic Monitor",
      "Amazon CloudWatch Internet Monitor",
      "Amazon CloudWatch Network Flow Monitor",
      "The Direct Connect Resiliency Toolkit failover test feature"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Network Synthetic Monitor sends synthetic probes (ICMP or TCP) from your VPC subnets to on-premises IP addresses and reports packet loss and round-trip latency over hybrid connections. AWS manages the probes, so no agents are needed on the servers.",
      "Internet Monitor measures internet traffic between applications and end users, not private hybrid links.",
      "Network Flow Monitor uses agents on EC2 instances to measure traffic between workloads in AWS. It does not probe on-premises destinations.",
      "The failover test brings down BGP sessions on purpose to check failover. It does not measure packet loss and latency continuously."] },

{ id: "d5-60", d: 5,
  q: "Remote employees connect to an AWS Client VPN endpoint successfully, but they cannot reach application servers in a private subnet of the associated VPC. The servers' security group allows traffic from the Client VPN endpoint's security group.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer check FIRST?",
  o: ["That an authorization rule allows the users' group to access the private subnet's CIDR range.",
      "That each employee's laptop has a public IP address that is registered in the VPC's route tables.",
      "That the private subnet has a NAT gateway so that the VPN clients can reach the private servers.",
      "That the Client VPN endpoint uses split tunneling so that all traffic goes through the VPN tunnel."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Client VPN authorization rules control which networks connected users can reach. Without a rule that allows the users, or their Active Directory or SAML group, to reach the subnet's CIDR range, connections succeed but traffic to that network is dropped. The Client VPN route table also needs a route to the destination.",
      "Client VPN assigns client addresses from its own CIDR range. Employees' public IP addresses do not go into VPC route tables.",
      "NAT gateways provide outbound internet access. VPN clients do not need them to reach private resources.",
      "Split tunneling sends only VPC-bound traffic through the VPN, which is the opposite of what this option says, and it does not grant access to subnets."] },

{ id: "d5-61", d: 5,
  q: "A Lambda function was attached to private subnets so that it can reach an Amazon RDS database. Since then, the function times out when it calls a third-party payment API on the internet.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Add a NAT gateway path for the function's private subnets, and keep the function in the VPC.",
      "Assign a public IP address to the function's network interfaces through the Lambda console.",
      "Move the function into a public subnet so that it uses the internet gateway route of the subnet.",
      "Increase the function timeout to 15 minutes so that the payment API call has time to complete."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lambda functions that are attached to a VPC get only private IP addresses, so they need a NAT gateway (zonal or regional) to reach the internet. Routing the function's subnets through NAT keeps database access and restores internet access.",
      "Lambda does not support public IP addresses on the network interfaces that it creates.",
      "Even in a public subnet, the function's network interfaces have no public IP addresses, so the internet gateway route does not help.",
      "The call fails because there is no network path, so a longer timeout only delays the failure."] },

{ id: "d5-62", d: 5,
  q: "A company uses a single AWS Direct Connect connection to reach its VPCs. During a recent fiber cut, all connectivity to AWS was lost for 6 hours. The company wants a low-cost backup path that takes over automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a Site-to-Site VPN to the same virtual private gateway or transit gateway, and use BGP for failover.",
      "Order a second Direct Connect connection in the same location on the same router at the same speed.",
      "Turn on Direct Connect SiteLink so that traffic moves to another Direct Connect location automatically.",
      "Ask the on-premises team to route traffic through the public internet to the instances' public IPs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Site-to-Site VPN over the internet is a low-cost backup for Direct Connect. When both use BGP with the same gateway, AWS prefers the Direct Connect path while it is up and fails over to the VPN automatically when the Direct Connect BGP session goes down.",
      "A second connection on the same router and in the same location shares the same failure points, and it costs more than a VPN.",
      "SiteLink connects on-premises sites to each other through Direct Connect locations. It does not back up a single failed connection.",
      "Routing to public IP addresses over the internet exposes resources and is not an automatic, private failover."] },

{ id: "d5-63", d: 5,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer tries to create a VPC peering connection between VPC A (10.0.0.0/16) and VPC B (10.0.128.0/17). The request fails.\n\nWhy does the request fail?",
  o: ["The CIDR blocks of the two VPCs overlap, and peering does not allow overlapping CIDR blocks.",
      "The two VPCs must be in different AWS Regions before any peering connection between them can be created.",
      "VPC peering requires that both VPCs use exactly the same CIDR block size, such as /16.",
      "VPC peering is allowed only between VPCs that are in two different AWS accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["10.0.128.0/17 falls inside 10.0.0.0/16, so the ranges overlap. A VPC peering connection cannot be created between VPCs with overlapping IPv4 CIDR blocks. Re-addressing one VPC, or using PrivateLink for specific services, solves this.",
      "Peering works both within a Region and across Regions.",
      "Peered VPCs can use different CIDR block sizes as long as the ranges do not overlap.",
      "Peering works between VPCs in the same account or in different accounts."] },

{ id: "d5-64", d: 5,
  q: "Tasks in an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate stop shortly after they start, and the team cannot see why. The task definition does not configure any logging.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to see the container's output?",
  o: ["Add the awslogs log driver to the task definition so that container output goes to CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Connect to the Fargate host with SSH, and read the Docker logs in the host's /var/log directory.",
      "Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the task's network interface to see the output that the container wrote.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for ECS so that each line of container output is stored in the trail."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The awslogs log driver, or FireLens for routing to other destinations, sends the container's stdout and stderr to CloudWatch Logs. The task's stopped reason and the container exit code in the ECS console also help explain why tasks stop. The task execution role needs permission to write the logs.",
      "Fargate hosts are managed by AWS, so there is no SSH access to them.",
      "Flow logs record network traffic metadata, not application output.",
      "CloudTrail records ECS API calls, not container output."] },

{ id: "d5-65", d: 5,
  q: "The operations team wants alarms when a CloudFront distribution's cache hit rate drops below 80% and when origin latency rises. The default CloudFront metrics in CloudWatch do not include these values.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Turn on additional metrics for the distribution, and alarm on CacheHitRate and OriginLatency in us-east-1.",
      "Turn on standard logging to S3, and create metric filters on the log files in the bucket for each value.",
      "Install the CloudWatch agent on the CloudFront edge servers to publish cache metrics for the distribution.",
      "Create the alarms in the Region of the origin, where CloudFront publishes its additional metrics by default."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront additional metrics, which have an extra cost, include CacheHitRate, OriginLatency, and error rates for each status code. CloudFront publishes its metrics in the US East (N. Virginia) Region, so the alarms must be created there.",
      "Metric filters work on CloudWatch Logs log groups, not on files in an S3 bucket.",
      "CloudFront edge servers are managed by AWS, and you cannot install agents on them.",
      "CloudFront publishes its metrics in us-east-1, not in the origin's Region."] },
]);
